veloper.nvidia.com】 【9†Sample Applications†github.com】\nL15: \nL16: Type\nL17: \nL18: 【5†All9】 【10†Tutorials8】 【11†Code Samples1】\nL19: \nL20: Primitive\nL21: 【5†All9】 【12†Big Integer3】 【13†NTT2】 【14†Hash Function2】 【15†Merkle Tree1】 【16†PQC (KEM / DSA)1】\nL22: # Tutorial: Staged NTT for Large Transforms\nL23: \nL24: This tutorial walks you through building a batched staged Number Theoretic Transform round-trip at N=16384 using cuPQC-NTT. You'll learn how `SubSize<M>` decomposes one large transform into two kernel passes, how to write the stage kernels for both directions, and how to size the shared memory and launch geometry each pass needs.\nL25: The NTT is the modular-arithmetic analogue of the FFT: it turns polynomial multiplication into cheap pointwise multiplication, which is why it sits on the critical path of lattice-based post-quantum schemes, fully homomorphic encryption, and zero-knowledge proof systems. A single-pass transform keeps its entire workspace in one block's shared memory, so it stops scaling once N grows past what a block can hold.\nL26: The staged API solves exactly that problem: with `SubSize<M>` and `K = N / M`, each pass bounds its per-block working set to `max(K, M)` elements rather than the full N, so the transform no longer has to fit in a single block at all.\nL27: cuPQC-NTT requires cuPQC SDK 0.6.0 or newer, the release that introduced it. cuPQC 0.6 targets compute capabilities 8.0, 8.6, 8.7, 8.9, and 9.0. The library implements the cyclic transform, meaning arithmetic in the ring modulo x^{N} - 1.\nL28: ## Step 1: Project Setup\nL29: \nL30: Clone the cuPQC repository:\nL31: \nL32:     `git clone https://github.com/NVIDIA/cuPQC.git\nL33:     cd cuPQC/examples/ntt\nL34:     `\nL35: \nL36: This will download all examples including the staged NTT example. The Makefile in this directory will compile all examples, including `example_staged_ntt`.\nL37: ## Step 2: Include Required Headers\nL38: \nL39: Start by including the necessary cuPQC SDK headers:\nL40: \nL41:     `#include <cstdlib>\nL42:     #include <vector>\nL43:     #include <iostream>\nL44: \nL45:     #include <ntt.hpp>\nL46: \nL47:     using namespace cupqc;\nL48:     `\nL49: \nL50: The `ntt.hpp` header provides the NTT descriptor operators, the staged transform entry points, the built-in prime fields, and the precomputed roots of unity.\nL51: ## Step 3: Understand the Staged Decomposition\nL52: \nL53: Choose N and the sub-size M up front, since both go into the descriptor as compile-time constants:\nL54: \nL55:     `constexpr uint32_t NTT_N = 16384;\nL56:     constexpr uint32_t NTT_M = 256;\nL57:     constexpr uint32_t NTT_K = NTT_N / NTT_M;  // 64\nL58:     `\nL59: \nL60: `SubSize<M>` splits an N-point NTT into two successive kernel passes with `K = N / M`:\nL61:   * Forward stage 1 runs `M` blocks, each performing a `K`-point transform over contiguous elements.\nL62:   * Forward stage 2 runs `K` blocks, each performing an `M`-point transform over strided elements.\nL63: The inverse reverses the stage ordering — its stage 1 is the `M`-point strided pass and its stage 2 is the `K`-point contiguous pass — and applies the `1/N` normalization in stage 2. Neither pass ever needs more than `max(K, M)` elements of shared memory, which for N=16384 with M=256 means 256 elements instead of 16384.\nL64: Staged transforms cover `2^14` through `2^24` points, and the allowed `SubSize<M>` values narrow as N grows, so consult the feature documentation before committing to a pair. This example uses N = 16384 = 2^{14}, split as M = 256, K = 64.\nL65: ## Step 4: Define the Staged NTT Types\nL66: Define one descriptor per direction. The staged behavior comes entirely from adding `SubSize<M>()` to an otherwise ordinary block-execution NTT descriptor:\nL67: \nL68:     `using ForwardNTT = decltype(Algorithm<algorithm::NTT>()\nL69:                                 + Direction<nttDirection::FORWARD>()\nL70:                                 + Precision<uint32_t>()\nL71:                                 + Size<NTT_N>()\nL72:                                 + SubSize<NTT_M>()\nL73:                                 + Block()\nL74:                                 + BlockDim<128>());\nL75: \nL76:     using InverseNTT = decltype(Algorithm<algorithm::NTT>()\nL77:                                 + Direction<nttDirection::INVERSE>()\nL78:                                 + Precision<uint32_t>()\nL79:                                 + Size<NTT_N>()\nL80:                                 + SubSize<NTT_M>()\nL81:                                 + Block()\nL82:                                 + BlockDim<128>());\nL83:     `\nL84: `Algorithm<algorithm::NTT>()` selects the transform.\nL85: `Direction<nttDirection::FORWARD>()` and `Direction<nttDirection::INVERSE>()` pick the direction; the two descriptors are otherwise identical.\nL86: `Precision<uint32_t>()` selects the coefficient and modulus width. `uint16_t`, `uint32_t`, and `uint64_t` are supported.\nL87: `Size<NTT_N>()` sets the transform length.\nL88: `SubSize<NTT_M>()` requests the staged decomposition with sub-size M, which is what makes the stage entry points available.\nL89: `Block()` with `BlockDim<128>()` requests block execution with 128 threads per block.\nL90: The descriptors expose the constants used later on: `ForwardNTT::Size` is N and `ForwardNTT::BlockDim` is the thread count, so the launch code never has to repeat them.\nL91: Both examples in this directory use KoalaBear, `p = 2^31 - 2^24 + 1`, which supports NTT sizes up to 2^{24}:\nL92: \nL93:     `constexpr uint32_t p = cupqc::KoalaBear;                    // KoalaBear prime\nL94:     constexpr uint32_t g = cupqc::KoalaBear_primitive_root_14;  // primitive 2^14-th root of unity mod p\nL95: \nL96:     const uint32_t g_inv = host_modpow(g, p - 2, p);  // g^{-1} mod p\nL97:     const uint32_t N_inv = 2130576385;                // (2^14)^{-1} mod p\nL98:     `\nL99: `KoalaBear_primitive_root_14` is one of the precomputed `*_primitive_root_S` constants for transform size 2^{S}. The inverse root is derived on the host with a small modular-exponentiation helper (`host_modpow`) using Fermat's little theorem, and `N_inv` is the `1/N` factor the inverse transform needs.\nL100: ## Step 5: Generate the Twiddle Tables\nL101: Twiddle generation is identical to the single-pass transform — the staged decomposition reuses the same tables across both of its passes:\nL102: \nL103:     `template<class NTT>\nL104:     __global__ void make_twiddles_kernel(uint32_t* twiddles, const uint32_t p, const uint32_t g) {\nL105:         NTT().make_twiddles(twiddles, p, g);\nL106:     }\nL107: \nL108:     template<class NTT>\nL109:     __global__ void transform_twiddles_to_mont_kernel(uint32_t* twiddles, const uint32_t p) {\nL110:         NTT().transform_twiddles_to_mont(twiddles, p);\nL111:     }\nL112:     `\nL113: Generate one table per direction and convert both into the Montgomery domain. This happens once and the tables are then reused for the entire batch:\nL114: \nL115:     `const nttConst<uint32_t> scheme_const(p);\nL116: \nL117:     make_twiddles_kernel<ForwardNTT><<<1, 1>>>(d_twiddles,     p, g);\nL118:     make_twiddles_kernel<InverseNTT><<<1, 1>>>(d_inv_twiddles, p, g_inv);\nL119:     transform_twiddles_to_mont_kernel<ForwardNTT><<<1, ForwardNTT::BlockDim>>>(d_twiddles, p);\nL120:     transform_twiddles_to_mont_kernel<InverseNTT><<<1, InverseNTT::BlockDim>>>(d_inv_twiddles, p);\nL121:     `\nL122: The forward table is built from `g`, the inverse table from `g^-1 mod p`. `nttConst<uint32_t>` bundles the modulus and its derived Montgomery constants; it is constructed on the host from `p` and passed to the kernels by value.\nL123: ## Step 6: Write the Forward Stage Kernels\nL124: Each stage is its own kernel, and each follows the same load, execute, store pattern as the single-pass transform, with stage-qualified entry points. Both kernels take the polynomial base pointer and a `batch_id`, and use `extern __shared__` so the workspace size can be chosen per stage at launch time:\nL125: \nL126:     `__global__ void fwd_stage_1_kernel(uint32_t* data, const uint32_t* twiddles,\nL127:                                        const nttConst<uint32_t> scheme_const, const int batch_id) {\nL128:         extern __shared__ uint32_t sdata[];\nL129:         ForwardNTT().stage_1_load_to_mont(sdata, data + batch_id * ForwardNTT::Size, blockIdx.x, scheme_const);\nL130:         __syncthreads();\nL131:         ForwardNTT().stage_1_execute(sdata, twiddles, scheme_const.p);\nL132:         __syncthreads();\nL133:         ForwardNTT().stage_1_store(sdata, data + batch_id * ForwardNTT::Size, blockIdx.x);\nL134:     }\nL135: \nL136:     __global__ void fwd_stage_2_kernel(uint32_t* data, const uint32_t* twiddles,\nL137:                                        const nttConst<uint32_t> scheme_const, const int batch_id) {\nL138:         extern __shared__ uint32_t sdata[];\nL139:         ForwardNTT().stage_2_load(sdata, data + batch_id * ForwardNTT::Size, blockIdx.x);\nL140:         __syncthreads();\nL141:         ForwardNTT().stage_2_execute(sdata, twiddles, scheme_const.p);\nL142:         __syncthreads();\nL143:         ForwardNTT().stage_2_store_from_mont(sdata, data + batch_id * ForwardNTT::Size, blockIdx.x, scheme_const);\nL144:     }\nL145:     `\nL146: Device transforms operate on Montgomery-domain data, so conversion happens at the memory boundary rather than per operation. Notice where that boundary falls across the two passes: stage 1 converts on the way in with `stage_1_load_to_mont` but stores with plain `stage_1_store`, and stage 2 reads with plain `stage_2_load` and only converts back out with `stage_2_store_from_mont`.\nL147: The intermediate result written to global memory between the passes therefore stays in the Montgomery domain, and the round trip through global memory costs no conversions.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGPU-